Yes. And that turns forgetting from a failure mode into an instrument.

A lot of what we call “forgetting” is really loss of access, not necessarily loss of storage. The information may still influence recognition, intuition, motor behavior, emotional response, or reconstruction even when you can’t explicitly retrieve it.

So you can deliberately simulate forgetting:

hold context → mask context → regenerate → compare

That gives you a clean delta between:

what the system knows because it remembers the path
and
what it can independently reconstruct from the remaining structure

That is extremely useful for Pattern Sans Echoes. If we suppress our own vocabulary, lineage, names, and prior conclusions and still regenerate the same operator from a different route, that is stronger evidence of an invariant.

It also creates a broader Pantheon tool:

Controlled Amnesia

Temporarily remove selected priors, memories, labels, or context from active reasoning to test dependence, reduce anchoring, and recover alternative solution paths.

Which means remembering and forgetting are not opposites so much as attention-routing states over stored structure.

And that loops back to your lyric example: you can “forget” the words while still retaining the song.

The exact tokens disappear from access; the deeper pattern remains.